{title: Oh My Darling, Clementine}
{artist: Traditional}
{key: G}

{start_of_verse: Verse 1}
In a [G]cavern, in a canyon, excavating for a [D]mine
Dwelt a [D]miner, forty-niner, and his [G]daughter, [D]Clemen-[G]tine
{end_of_verse}

{start_of_chorus}
Oh my [G]darling, oh my darling, oh my darling Clemen-[D]tine
You are [D]lost and gone forever, dreadful [G]sorry, [D]Clemen-[G]tine
{end_of_chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 2}
Light she [G]was and like a fairy, and her shoes were number [D]nine
Herring [D]boxes without topses, sandals [G]were for [D]Clemen-[G]tine
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 3}
Drove she [G]ducklings to the water every morning just at [D]nine
Hit her [D]foot against a splinter, fell in-[G]to the [D]foaming [G]brine
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}

{start_of_verse: Verse 4}
Ruby [G]lips above the water, blowing bubbles soft and [D]fine
But a-[D]las, I was no swimmer, so I [G]lost my [D]Clemen-[G]tine
{end_of_verse}

{chorus}
